I. INTRODUCTION
DRIVER monitoring system (DMS) initially focused on detecting driver behaviors such as steering, braking, and acceleration to address potential accidents [1]. However, these methods have limitations in effectively addressing preventable human factors, such as driver fatigue and distraction [2]. Recognizing the need for further advancements, recent research has introduced the integration of vital sign monitoring as a new option in DMS. Vital signs, including heart rate (HR) [3], respiratory rate (RR) [4], HR variability (HRV) [4], and blood pressure (BP) [5], provide valuable insights into the driver’s physical condition. By continuously monitoring these physiological indicators, the system can detect fatigue [6], distraction [7] and other physiological changes [8], [9], [10], [11] that may significantly impact driving performance. This integration of vital sign monitoring enhances the proactive identification of potential risks and enables the implementation of preventive measures, thus improving overall road safety.
Traditional HR measurement techniques involve the use of contact-based sensors like electrocardiography (ECG) and pulse oximeters [12]. While these methods provide accurate HR measurements, direct skin contact can cause skin irritation to drivers and potentially interfere with driving [6]. Remote photoplethysmography (rPPG) has emerged as a prominent noncontact HR measurement method. By capturing subtle color changes in the skin caused by subcutaneous capillary variations, rPPG extracts pulse signals from facial videos, enabling HR detection [13].
However, for common near-infrared (NIR) monitoring in DMS, most existing rPPG algorithms face challenges. First, NIR images utilized in DMS are monochromatic, resulting in a lower signal-to-noise ratio of extracted rPPG signals [14], [15], rendering RGB-based methods [3], [16], [17], [18] using three color channels inapplicable. Compared to the RGB- and NIR-based methods, the RGB image has better antimotion interference ability [19], while the NIR image has stronger antilight interference ability [20]. Although RGB-depth technology [21] has the advantage of detection accuracy, there are certain obstacles in the application of DMS, such as the difficulty of model deployment and ensuring real-time performance. Second, there are unique sources of noise in moving vehicles, which can be categorized into two aspects: 1) the uneven distribution of ambient light on the driver’s face during driving, such as the variations caused by buildings and trees on the sides of the daytime road or changes in

