In recent years, several rPPG algorithms in NIR have been proposed to overcome these challenges. Zhang et al. [22] proposed a method for monitoring and analyzing driver HR using empirical mode decomposition, bandpass filtering, and fast Fourier transformation. They achieved excellent detection results in complex lighting environments. However, they only considered the impact of lighting and lacked testing in realistic driving scenarios. Nowara et al. [23] introduced the SparsePPG algorithm for driver monitoring using in-car cameras, which enables HR detection in both NIR and RGB modalities. They employed narrowband NIR videos and an active NIR illumination system to reduce variations in ambient light and utilized robust principal component analysis (RPCA) for reliable HR measurements. Building upon their previous work, the same team proposed the AutoSparsePPG algorithm in 2020 [24]. This algorithm leverages the quasiperiodicity of rPPG signals to enhance the robustness of HR detection against motion interference. It is important to note that both these methods utilize narrowband NIR solutions with monochrome cameras. They select 48 regions of interest (ROIs) on the face and calculate the mean color values of these ROIs to extract the rPPG signal. However, since noise affects different ROIs to varying degrees, it is uncertain which ROIs produce reliable signals in the presence of motion interference and variations in lighting conditions. Hernandez-Ortega et al. [25] introduced a signal quality metric in NIR rPPG to assess the quality of the signal by calculating a signal quality score. This metric helps in identifying cases of inaccurate HR estimation. However, the signal quality metric was primarily used for selecting accurate or inaccurate signal segments and did not directly contribute to the HR detection process itself. It did not address the challenges of motion artifacts and variations in lighting conditions.
In previous research, we have acknowledged the inevitable impact of motion interference on rPPG, leading to the presence of residual motion noise [26]. This impact is manifested as waveform distortion in the time domain and multipeak spectra in the frequency domain of rPPG signals. Furthermore, the extent of this interference varies among different signals. In this article, we introduce an innovative algorithm to address the previously mentioned challenges and issues arising from residual noise. Our method incorporates the quality-guided spectrum peak screening (QSPS) algorithm, which integrates the quality characteristics of pulse signals to achieve more accurate HR estimation and assessment.
The reason for peak screening during the HR estimation process lies in the fact that motion interference introduces additional frequency components that may not necessarily

correspond to the actual HR frequency. This issue arises from the fact that motion disturbances introduce extra frequency components that can interfere with the HR signal, leading to inaccuracies in HR estimation. Specifically, residual motion noise can result in multiple peaks in the spectrum of the rPPG signal, and these peaks may not correspond to the actual heartbeats. These additional peaks may be caused by facial movements, changes in lighting conditions, or other external factors rather than cardiac activity.
The proposed method offers three key contributions.
1) We propose a dedicated HR monitoring system designed specifically for automotive cabin environments. Our method is aimed at extracting rPPG signals from a single-channel 940-nm narrowband NIR source, enabling precise monitoring of drivers’ HR. Importantly, we take into consideration the diverse manifestations of residual noise in different signal domains, effectively mitigating the adverse effects of residual noise on HR detection.
2) We introduce an extensive framework for evaluating the quality of pulse signals. By extracting rich quality information embedded within pulse signals, we can ascertain whether the signal is predominantly influenced by frequency interference. This quality information serves as a guiding tool to enhance the precision of HR detection.
3) Differing from sole reliance on quality assessment for output and confidence estimation, we extensively leverage signal quality information to conduct spectral screening of pulse signals across various facial regions. By utilizing the QSPS algorithm, our method effectively identifies spectral distortions induced by motion interference or lighting artifacts. This screening process minimizes the impact of motion interference on HR estimation, resulting in outcomes that are not only more accurate but also more reliable.
