B. Residual Motion Noise
The pulse signal generally demonstrates greater stability compared to the occasional rapid fluctuations caused by noise. During vigorous physical activities, motion-induced interference usually encompasses both high- and low-frequency components. This arises from diverse muscle contractions and movements involved in rapid motions, resulting in motion signals with a broad energy distribution across the frequency domain. We categorize motion-induced interference as M(t), comprising a high-frequency component Mh(t) and a low-frequency component Ml (t) that closely aligns in frequency with the HR

M(t) = Mh(t) + Ml (t).

(6)

In (5), high-frequency components and constant terms can be eliminated, resulting in the preliminarily denoised signal rPPG(t) represented as

rPPG(t) = vd (t)Ml (t)[I0 + Iamb(t)].

