When the low-frequency component Ml (t) approaches the HR frequency or its harmonics, it might coincide with the frequency components of the HR signal, introducing additional peaks or ripples in the frequency spectrum. This interference affects the accuracy of HR estimation, leading to residual motion noise. We will elucidate the impact of residual motion noise on the time–frequency characteristics of the pulse signal and discuss how to mitigate its influence on HR estimation through algorithmic enhancements in the subsequent Section III-C.
In real-world driving scenarios, substantial fluctuations in ambient light and motion are unavoidable, contributing to the presence of residual noise. However, the extent to which they affect the frequency spectrum after extracting the pulse signal can vary, leading to diverse HR estimation outcomes. The challenge of being impacted by residual motion noise is prevalent in existing methods. The objective of this study is to address the issue of detecting motion anomalies during driving and reducing the impact of residual motion noise on HR estimation.

