C. Quality-Guided Spectrum Peak Screening
The QSPS algorithm aims to address the challenges associated with inaccurate HR estimation in rPPG signals obtained from NIR imaging. These challenges arise from the low signalto-noise ratio and various sources of interference, which can dominate the frequency spectrum obtained through the Fourier transform (FFT) and lead to erroneous calculations.
rPPG signals, being weak biological signals, can exhibit multiple peaks in certain cases, which can complicate HR estimation. These multiple peaks are often caused by residual motion noise and other sources of interference. Residual noise refers to the remaining noise components within the HR frequency range (typically 0.7–4 Hz) even after removing irregular and high-frequency noise. These noise components can dominate the spectrum and introduce multiple peaks in the rPPG signal. The presence of residual motion noise is particularly prominent in segments with poor signal quality, where noise frequencies can overshadow the HR, leading to inaccurate estimation. One scenario that can introduce noise-dominated signals is motion artifacts, as shown in 1(d). During daily activities, head and body movements generate vibrations that propagate to the face and impact the rPPG signal. These motion artifacts introduce motion-induced peaks, making it difficult to accurately extract the underlying HR information. Another scenario that can lead to noise-dominated signals is varying illumination conditions. Changes in lighting intensity can cause fluctuations in the rPPG signal, leading to erroneous peak estimation. These fluctuations can arise from factors such as changes in ambient light, shadows, or variations in the positioning of the light source. Furthermore, motion blur can also contribute to noise-dominated signals in rPPG. When there is rapid motion or movement during video recording, it can result in blurred frames, making it challenging to accurately capture the subtle changes in skin color associated with the cardiac cycle.
To overcome these issues, the QSPS algorithm introduces a novel approach that integrates signal quality assessment with spectrum analysis. By considering the signal quality information, the algorithm selectively retains peaks in the spectrum that are most likely to originate from the HR signal while filtering out noise-dominated peaks. This integration ensures that the HR estimation is based on reliable and informative spectral components, enhancing the accuracy of the results.
