The assessment comprises two main parts, as shown in Fig. 3.
1) Spectral Features: In the first part, the pulse signal collection undergoes FFT to convert to the frequency domain, revealing its spectrum. We calculate four spectral features for each window signal. Furthermore, this assessment encompasses spectrum characteristics to evaluate rPPG signal quality, focusing on four key features.
1) Main Bandwidth (MBW): This feature represents the bandwidth centered around the dominant frequency component in the frequency spectrum, encompassing a significant portion of signal energy.
2) Enhanced Signal-to-Noise Ratio (ESNR): ESNR is defined as the ratio of the power at the dominant frequency component in the frequency spectrum to the power in the remaining frequency range. It quantifies the strength of the desired signal relative to background noise.
3) Peak Ratio (PR): PR indicates the prominence of the dominant frequency component.
4) Peak Sharpness (PS): This feature reflects the sharpness of the spectral peak.
