V. DISCUSSION
In our experiments, we have demonstrated the robustness of our algorithm in narrowband NIR settings, showcasing its resilience to changing ambient lighting and low-light conditions in the presence of minimal motion. Even under significant motion, our algorithm accurately predicted HR values that closely approximated the GT. While achieving precise HR detection in all challenging scenarios remains a formidable task, our primary focus lies in evaluating signal quality and selecting reliable HR values within specific time intervals. Consequently, when faced with severe interference that impedes accurate HR calculation, we can identify and exclude outliers with substantial deviations using quality scores. Our algorithm incorporates the QSPS technique, leveraging signal quality features to optimize HR detection. Through signal quality assessment, we effectively discriminate reliable HR values from those distorted by interference, resulting in enhanced accuracy and reliability. Moreover, our spectrum screening step adeptly identifies spectral distortions attributed to motion artifacts or lighting interference, subsequently allowing us to exclude such instances and achieve more accurate measurements.
In comparison to other algorithms, our approach offers notable advantages. First, it strikes a balance between real-time detection and prolonged continuous monitoring demands. This balance ensures the real-time tracking of HR fluctuations while also facilitating stable HR estimation over extended periods. Second, our algorithm excels in driving scenarios, effectively addressing a spectrum of challenging situations while maintaining high accuracy and reliability in both daytime and nighttime driving conditions.
Notwithstanding the algorithm’s commendable performance across diverse scenarios, we acknowledge its limitations. In extreme cases marked by intense motion interference or exceedingly low lighting, precise HR estimation may prove unattainable. In such scenarios, we recommend employing quality assessment scores to filter results and ensure the selection of highly reliable HR values. Additionally, considering the specificity of our experimental driving scenarios, algorithm performance may vary in disparate driving environments.
VI. CONCLUSION
This study presents a comprehensive analysis of residual noise’s impact on narrowband NIR rPPG and introduces algorithmic enhancements to mitigate this challenge. By integrating quality assessment as an auxiliary tool, our approach effectively addresses the issue of multipeak spectra resulting from residual noise. The practical deployment of our system within a real-world driver monitoring setup has yielded promising outcomes. Through real-world driving scenarios, we have verified the effectiveness and reliability of our

algorithm under realistic conditions. The integration of our system with an enterprise-level DMS enables real-time monitoring of drivers’ physiological indicators during both day and night driving. Our system accurately measures HR and provides immediate feedback, thereby aiding in the continual monitoring of the driver’s physical and mental well-being.
Regarding future directions for improvement, we have identified two potential avenues. First, one potential avenue for improvement involves integrating external environmental factors that could impact signal quality into the assessment process. This could be achieved through the application of machine learning to optimize the scoring and classification process, thereby enhancing the overall accuracy of the assessment. Second, optimizing the process of pulse signal extraction and exploring innovative regional pulse extraction algorithms, especially within challenging driving scenarios characterized by varying motion and lighting conditions, presents another promising direction for enhancement. These advancements have the potential to further elevate the accuracy and reliability of HR estimation, thereby strengthening the effectiveness of our algorithm in addressing the challenges posed by real-world driving scenarios.
